mcprack for Debian
===================

Configuration lives in /etc/mcprack/env (create it before the first start —
see the systemd unit's EnvironmentFile). Required variables:

  SECRET_KEY=<random-string>
  SQLALCHEMY_DATABASE_URI=sqlite:////var/lib/mcprack/mcprack.db
  # or postgresql+psycopg2://... / mysql+pymysql://... (install python3-psycopg2
  # or python3-pymysql respectively — see Suggests in debian/control)

  LDAP_SERVER=ldap://10.11.25.3:389
  LDAP_BASE_DN=dc=spojent,dc=cz
  LDAP_USER_OU=ou=Users,dc=spojent,dc=cz
  LDAP_USER_FILTER=(sAMAccountName=%(user)s)

  BW_SERVER=https://vaultwarden-dev.proxy.spojenet.cz
  BW_CLIENTID=...
  BW_CLIENTSECRET=...
  BW_PASSWORD=...
  BITWARDENCLI_APPDATA_DIR=/var/lib/mcprack/.bw

Get BW_CLIENTID/BW_CLIENTSECRET from the Vaultwarden web vault: Account
Settings -> Security -> Keys (API Key). BW_PASSWORD is that same account's
master password. After setting these, log in to mcprack as an admin and open
"Vaultwarden diagnostics" in the nav bar (/admin/vaultwarden/wizard) — it
checks the bw binary, BW_SERVER reachability, the API key, and the master
password one at a time and tells you exactly which one is still wrong,
instead of a raw "bw login failed" error. Re-run it after every edit to
/etc/mcprack/env; no service restart is needed.

After creating /etc/mcprack/env, run the database migration once:

  su -s /bin/sh www-data -c 'cd /usr/lib/mcprack && FLASK_APP=app:create_app flask db upgrade'

Then create the first admin account:

  su -s /bin/sh www-data -c 'cd /usr/lib/mcprack && FLASK_APP=app:create_app flask create-admin'

Finally start the service:

  systemctl enable --now mcprack


MCP HTTP Proxy (for remote access to stdio servers)
====================================================

If you want remote clients (e.g., Claude Desktop running on a different
workstation) to connect to stdio-based MCP servers running on this host, you
need to expose them over HTTP. mcprack includes a systemd service that uses
fastmcp to proxy all registered stdio servers.

1. Install fastmcp (recommended, not required):

     apt install python3-fastmcp

2. In the mcprack admin UI (/admin/servers), for each stdio server you want
   to expose remotely:

   a) Leave "Command" filled in (e.g., /usr/bin/mastodon-mcp)
   b) Fill in "URL" as: http://YOUR-HOST-IP:3100/mcp/
      (e.g., http://10.11.182.99:3100/mcp/)
   c) Set "Auth header name" and "Auth env key" if your server needs
      authentication tokens

   This tells mcprack: "This server runs via stdio locally, but remote users
   should connect to it via this HTTP endpoint."

3. Enable and start the proxy service:

     systemctl enable --now mcprack-proxy.service

   The proxy will:
   - Read all enabled stdio servers from the database
   - Generate /var/lib/mcprack/proxy-mcp.json
   - Start fastmcp on 0.0.0.0:3100 to expose them over HTTP

4. When users download their config (e.g., Claude or Copilot), they'll
   automatically get network entries pointing to http://YOUR-HOST:3100/mcp/
   instead of local stdio commands.

5. Optional: Set additional environment variables for the proxy in
   /etc/mcprack/proxy.env (e.g., API tokens needed by the MCP servers).

6. To restart the proxy after adding/removing servers:

     systemctl restart mcprack-proxy.service

Note: The proxy port (3100) must be accessible from client workstations. Make
sure your firewall allows incoming connections on that port.
